INT. ABANDONED DRIVE-IN PROJECTION BOOTH - DUSK

Dust hangs in the last orange light like a held breath. A cracked projector squats in the middle of the room, and through the small square window the huge blank screen glows pink, empty of everything but weeds at its feet.

ROSALIND "ROZ" BEAUMONT, sixty-eight, runs a thumb along the projector's lens cap as if checking a sleeping animal for a pulse. Her grandson, KWAME ASANTE-BEAUMONT, fifteen, stands in the doorway with a duffel bag he has not put down.

KWAME
Mum said you'd be mad.

ROZ
Your mother's been saying that since 1994. I've never once been mad on schedule.

He lets the bag slide to the floor. She does not turn around.

ROZ (CONT'D)
You want to see something?

She flips a rusted switch. Nothing. She flips it again. The projector coughs, and a long, trembling beam of white light leaps out the window and lands on the screen like a thrown door.

KWAME
It works?

ROZ
It does not. That's the sun, in the glass. Always happens at dusk.

They both watch the shape of the light slide down the screen and fade.

KWAME
I'm sorry about Grandad.

ROZ
Mm.

KWAME ^
I'm sorry I didn't come.

ROZ ^
Sit down, you're blocking my view.

He sits. She finally turns, and puts a hand briefly on his hair.
